; stringutil.IsWhiteSpaceLike: it is ECMAScript's LineTerminator plus WhiteSpace,
; and WhiteSpace pulls in the whole Zs category — of which U+0085, the one the
; scanner's own single-line set carries, is NOT a member.
function jsnum_is_zs(r: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked
















function jsnum_is_str_white_space(r: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked






















function jsnum_is_digit(c: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked


function jsnum_is_number_rune(r: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked






















function jsnum_digit_value(c: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked








function jsnum_all_digits_base(s: Slice[char], off: int, slen: int, base: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked




























; The number of DECIMAL digits this port will build for a radix literal. A value
; of 400 decimal digits is at least 10^399, so anything longer is past the
; largest double and answers +Inf — which is what big.Int's Float64 answers for
; it too.
define JSNUM_RADIX_DEC_CAP: int 400

; The exact integer a radix literal names, as an IEEE bit pattern.
;
; ★★★ THE REFERENCE SPLITS THIS IN TWO AND THE SPLIT IS NOT OBSERVABLE.
; `tryParseInt` reads the digits with `strconv.ParseInt(rest, base, 64)` and
; converts the int64 to a Number, and on RANGE error falls back to
; `big.Int.SetString(s, 0)` and `Float64()`. Both are "the exact integer, rounded
; to the nearest double, ties to even" — Go's int64→float64 conversion is
; specified as round-to-even and big.Float's is too — so ONE conversion answers
; both arms, and this port builds the exact decimal expansion and hands it to the
; same `dec_set`/`dec_float_bits` pair every other value goes through.
function jsnum_radix_to_bits(s: Slice[char], off: int, slen: int, base: int) @deep true returns u64 linked













































; tryParseInt, reduced to its three RADIX arms.
;
; ★★★ THE DECIMAL ARM IS PROVABLY THE GENERAL PATH and is therefore not written
; twice. It trims leading zeros, tests `isAllDigits` and parses base 10 — and the
; float route that follows a `false` answer does exactly the same thing to exactly
; the same strings: `parseFloatString` trims the same leading zeros and rejects
; the same non-digits, and `ParseFloat` of an integer string is the exact integer
; rounded to nearest-even, which is what int64→float64 is. The RADIX arms are the
; only ones the float route cannot answer, because `x`, `o` and `b` are not
; digits, so those are the only ones here.
procedure jsnum_try_parse_int(s: Slice[char], off: int, slen: int, mutable out_bits: ref[u64]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

























; jsnum.FromString — ECMAScript's StringToNumber, answering the double's BITS.
;
; ★★ NO SEPARATOR EVER REACHES THIS, AND `decimal.set`'s TWO `_` ARMS ARE DEAD
; CODE ON BOTH SIDES. The scanner removes them while it builds the raw value,
; which is where the reference removes them too — and even if one got through,
; `isNumberRune` below does not admit `_`, so the answer would be NaN before any
; decimal was built. The arms are ported because `decimal.set` is ported arm for
; arm, and in Go they ARE live: its other caller is `ParseFloat`, whose
; `readFloat` validates separators first, which is what their comment there says.
; Measured rather than argued: slice 44's c9 removes them and is UNGATED on the
; four yardsticks AND on tests/numcheck.sh, which feeds jsnum raw strings.
;
; ★★ PARSE-FLOAT-STRING IS NOT PORTED and the equivalence is exact rather than
; approximate. It cuts the string at `.` and `e`, trims leading zeros from the
; integer part and trailing zeros from the fraction, substitutes "0" for an empty
; integer or fraction part, normalises the exponent's sign and leading zeros, and
; rejects anything that is then not all digits — and every one of those is
; something `decimal.set` already does to the ORIGINAL string: it ignores leading
; zeros (decrementing dp), it tracks the point itself so a trailing `.` and an
; absent fraction are the same number, it reads the exponent's sign and clamps its
; magnitude, and it answers `false` on the first byte that belongs to none of
; those, which is the NaN the rewrite would have produced. The two shapes where
; the STRING this port hands over differs from the reference's — a kept leading
; zero and a kept empty fraction, both only in the separator branch of the
; scanner — are absorbed by those same arms, and the VALUE is the same in each.
; Go's unary minus on a float64 (slice 102). The reference writes
; `-jsnum.FromString(text)` in checkPrefixUnaryExpression's numeric-literal arm and
; `+…` beside it; the plus is the identity and is not written.
;
; ★★★ IT IS THE SIGN BIT AND THAT IS EXACT, NOT AN APPROXIMATION. IEEE-754 defines
; negation as a bit flip of the sign for EVERY value including the zeros and the
; NaNs — the one operation on a float that is not an arithmetic rounding — so a
; port that went through a `0 - x` subtraction would answer `+0` for `-0` and lose
; the very literal the arm exists to mint. `-0` is a type of its own here: the
; number-literal intern table is keyed on the BITS.
function jsnum_negate(bits: u64) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns u64 linked


function jsnum_from_string(s: Slice[char]) @deep true returns u64 linked




















































































; Is this double a safe integer in the sense of Number.String's fast path —
; `MinSafeInteger <= n && n <= MaxSafeInteger && int64(n) == float64(n)` — and if
; so, what is |int64(n)|?
;
; ★★ THE FAST PATH IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION, it decides one answer: `int64(n)`
; loses the sign of NEGATIVE ZERO, so -0 prints "0" here and would print "-0"
; through the json encoder, whose own doc says so. ECMAScript's ToString(-0) is
; "0", so the fast path is the arm that makes this right.
;
; ★ The four cases are the bit pattern's, and each is the reference's condition
; read off it: a subnormal is an integer only when it is ±0; an exponent above the
; mantissa's width is at least 2^53 and so past MaxSafeInteger; at exactly the
; mantissa's width the mantissa IS the value; below it the value is an integer
; exactly when the bits it drops are zero.
procedure jsnum_safe_integer(bits: u64, mutable out_mag: ref[u64]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked




























procedure jsnum_format_uint(v: u64, mutable out: Slice[char]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked



























; jsnum.Number.String — the ECMAScript Number-to-String, written into `out` (at
; most JSNUM_MAX_TEXT bytes) and answering its length.
procedure jsnum_to_string(bits: u64, mutable out: Slice[char]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_into_1_2 true returns int linked
































































; ── PseudoBigInt ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; jsnum/pseudobigint.go's ParsePseudoBigInt, and it is the one routine slice 44
; left as a STAND-IN with its reader named: *"what would earn the port is the
; CHECKER's bigint literal type"*. That reader is slice 71's BigIntLiteral arm,
; so the stand-in expires here (§3.5p).
;
; ★★ THE OUTPUT IS A DIGIT STRING AND THE CALLER OWNS ITS STORAGE, which is the
; file's own shape for a routine whose answer does not fit a register — `Decimal`
; takes `pointer[char]` scratch the caller declares, and this takes the buffer the
; decimal digits are written into. Answers the LENGTH written.
;
; ★★★ THE CAPACITY THE CALLER OWES IS `slen + slen / 4 + 4` AND THAT BOUND IS THE
; WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT, because there is no way to signal an overflow that a
; caller could act on. The widest base is 16, and one hex digit is at most
; log10(16) = 1.20412 decimal digits; `slen` already counts the two prefix bytes
; and the `n`, so the digit count is at most `slen - 2` and the decimal length at
; most `1.20412 * (slen - 2) + 1 < 1.25 * slen`. Bases 2 and 8 shrink instead of
; growing, so the same bound covers all three.
;
; ★ The reference PANICS when `big.Int.SetString` refuses the text. Here the
; refusal is answered with -1: every caller's input has been through the scanner's
; own digit scan, so it is unreachable, and a trap would be a claim that the input
; is impossible rather than that no caller can produce it.
procedure jsnum_parse_pseudo_big_int(s: Slice[char], mutable out: Slice[char]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true @deep_into_1_3 true returns int linked


























































































































; ── the ARITHMETIC half of internal/jsnum (slice 116) ───────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE HEADER'S *this port never names Scaly's `double`* EXPIRES HERE, and the
; way it expires is §3.5p exactly: the sentence was true of what jsnum was being
; asked for, not of jsnum. Everything above converts BETWEEN a decimal string and
; an IEEE bit pattern, and Go's own conversion is integer arithmetic on a digit
; array — so naming a double there would have bought nothing and cost the exact
; agreement `tests/numcheck.sh` measures. What arrives with the enum chapter is
; the other half of the reference file: `x + y`, `x % y`, `x ** y`, the five
; bitwise operators and the three shifts, i.e. ECMAScript's Number OPERATIONS.
; Those are IEEE-754 double arithmetic by definition, and reimplementing them on
; the bit pattern would be writing a soft-float library to avoid a keyword.
;
; ★★ THE INTERFACE STAYS BITS AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Every value crossing into
; this file is a `u64` — `LiteralTypeData.bits`, `jsnum_from_string`'s answer,
; `jsnum_to_string`'s input — so a double lives only INSIDE a function here, and
; no caller of jsnum has to learn a second representation. The bridge is one
; pointer cast in each direction.
;
; ★★★ AND `<>` ON A double IS ORDERED, so it answers FALSE for NaN against
; itself. Measured: `if nan = nan` does not fire (right) and `if nan <> nan` does
; not fire either (wrong for JS) — the emitter's inequality is `fcmp one` where
; ECMAScript's `!==` is `une`. Nothing here may test NaN-ness with a comparison;
; `jsnum_is_nan` reads the BIT PATTERN, which is what the reference's own
; `isNonFinite` does one line further on for the same kind of reason.

function fmod(x: double, y: double) returns double extern
function pow(x: double, y: double) returns double extern
function trunc(x: double) returns double extern

; The bridge. A `var` of scalar type has a slot, so `&` addresses it and the cast
; reads the same eight bytes as the other type — the one shape in this package
; that is a reinterpretation rather than a conversion, and `n as double` (an
; int-to-float CONVERSION) is what it must not be confused with.
function jsnum_double_of(bits: u64) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns double linked






function jsnum_bits_of(value: double) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns u64 linked






; `isNonFinite`, transcribed: exponent field all ones. One mask instead of the
; two questions `math.IsNaN(x) || math.IsInf(x, 0)` asks, which is the comment
; the reference puts over it.
function jsnum_is_non_finite(bits: u64) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked


function jsnum_is_nan(bits: u64) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked






function jsnum_is_inf(bits: u64) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked






; math.Abs — the sign bit cleared, which is what Go's own implementation is.
