function jsnum_from_int(n: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns u64 linked


; int32 wrap-around — Go's `int32(int64(x))`, i.e. the low 32 bits read as a
; signed quantity. It is a separate function because ToInt32 and LeftShift both
; end in it and the second one would otherwise be off by a modulus.
function jsnum_wrap_int32(v: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked







; ToInt32 (tc39 §7.1.6), and the reference's FAST PATH is deliberately not here.
; It is `if smi := int32(x); float64(smi) == x { return smi }` — a conversion
; whose out-of-range behaviour Go leaves to the platform and LLVM makes POISON,
; so transcribing it would be the one shape in this file that is undefined rather
; than merely slow. ★It costs nothing in ANSWERS and that is provable rather than
; assumed: the fast path fires exactly when x is an exact int32, and for such an x
; the slow path computes trunc(x) = x, fmod(x, 2^32) = x (|x| < 2^32), and a
; low-32-bit read of x, which is x.
function jsnum_to_int32(bits: u64) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked







; ToUint32 — "the only difference is the interpretation of the bits", so it is
; ToInt32's answer read as an unsigned 32-bit quantity.
function jsnum_to_uint32(bits: u64) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns u64 linked


